Title: "Resource Headers"
Id: "0eabf9e6-6eaa-4ca5-ae55-da8d56eda95c"

Text: "Resource headers begin with `$` and are used by `Resource:` groups and `References:` sections.

```mlg-fragment
[$zfc.foundations]
Resource:
. title: \"ZFC Foundations\"
. url: \"https://example.com\"
```

A `References:` section names resources with these headers. Both item-wide bare
`$book.chapter` (or quoted `\"$book.chapter\"`) entries and label-specific
`for:`/`entry:` groups are accepted:

```mlg-fragment
References:
. $zfc.overview
. for: (:foundations:)
  entry: $zfc.foundations:page{42}
. for: (:analysis:)
  entry: $rudin.principles
```

Duplicate reference labels within the same `References:` section are rejected.

Labels can be defined on groups (`[label]`), lines (`. [label]: ...`), grouped formulations (`(x = x)[:analysis:]`), or quoted prose (&#39;&#39;quoted text&#39;&#39;[&#58;label&#58;]), and referenced in `References:` (`for: (:label:)`), `Justification:` (`for: (:label:)`), or prose (`(:label:)`).

Every label referenced by `for: (:label:)` in `References:` must match a label defined in the enclosing item; referencing an unknown label produces an error.

The resource's `offset:` is the physical PDF page on which its logical page 1
appears, so the linked physical page is `n + offset - 1` (`offset` defaults to
`1`).

The viewer displays a resolved reference as the resource's title followed by its
author entries in parentheses, linking to the resource's `url:` when it has one.
Citations in prose and formulations render as interactive hyperlinks to the referenced resource.
`References:` is supporting content and is collapsed by default alongside
`Documented:` and `Id:`."
Id: "4a1ddf88-4cdb-4ef9-a0e8-c71830b26f68"
